{"findings":[{"id":"4209e603-e565-40bb-b9d9-7754cb40e5a2","title":"A later tap's no-match message is hidden","description":"`pushed` resets `said`, but a delayed `retry` for an earlier push sets it again because the handler checks only the device ID, not the push times in `d.pushes`. If the later push was silently dropped, its own timer emits `nomatch`, but `!said` is false and the earlier push's sticky “Try again” message remains.","category":"Correctness","action_level":"remediation_recommended","attribution_status":"pending","git_sha":"1306dc9550075866fb556726e87e1526b70fe4b9","location":{"file_path":"web/index.html","start_line":377,"end_line":380,"side":"right"},"evidence":{"citations":[{"source_type":"pr_branch_file","source":"web/index.html[370-380]"},{"source_type":"pr_branch_file","source":"web/net.js[133-137]"},{"source_type":"pr_branch_file","source":"web/net.js[158-164]"}]}},{"id":"0f2994d4-afd4-4c73-88c3-95fb9f2b1a9a","title":"The retry instruction disappears early","description":"The `retry` handler calls `toast()`, whose existing timeout hides its contents after 2.4 seconds regardless of `triedAgain`. When a device is told to try again and makes no further push, the instruction disappears rather than remaining visible until the next tap.","category":"Correctness","action_level":"remediation_recommended","attribution_status":"implemented","git_sha":"1306dc9550075866fb556726e87e1526b70fe4b9","location":{"file_path":"web/index.html","start_line":370,"end_line":372,"side":"right"},"evidence":{"citations":[{"source_type":"pr_branch_file","source":"web/index.html[65-65]"},{"source_type":"pr_branch_file","source":"web/index.html[365-373]"},{"source_type":"pr_branch_file","source":"web/index.html[24-25]"}]}},{"id":"e5fa90a2-f25f-4c2d-9c2e-0aabc032907d","title":"A second push can receive no result","description":"`result()` clears the device’s single `noMatchTimer` whenever any result names that device, without identifying which push received the result. If a device pushes twice and one push gets a result while the other is dropped from a full batch, the first result cancels the second push’s only fallback.","category":"Correctness","action_level":"remediation_recommended","attribution_status":"implemented","git_sha":"1306dc9550075866fb556726e87e1526b70fe4b9","location":{"file_path":"web/net.js","start_line":132,"end_line":133,"side":"right"},"evidence":{"citations":[{"source_type":"pr_branch_file","source":"web/layout.js[124-128]"},{"source_type":"pr_branch_file","source":"web/net.js[99-102]"},{"source_type":"pr_branch_file","source":"web/net.js[131-134]"},{"source_type":"pr_branch_file","source":"web/net.js[155-161]"}]}},{"id":"c7362445-a6d5-4315-9baa-19adb76c1795","title":"A successful join shows as no match","description":"The join-message handler resets `triedAgain` on `joined` but allows a subsequent `nomatch` for the same device to replace the success message. If a device pushes twice and only one push pairs, `resolve()` can announce the join and then the expired unmatched push’s `nomatch` in that order, leaving the joining device with the wrong result.","category":"Correctness","action_level":"remediation_recommended","attribution_status":"implemented","git_sha":"1306dc9550075866fb556726e87e1526b70fe4b9","location":{"file_path":"web/index.html","start_line":368,"end_line":373,"side":"right"},"evidence":{"citations":[{"source_type":"pr_branch_file","source":"web/layout.js[123-138]"},{"source_type":"pr_branch_file","source":"web/layout.js[159-173]"},{"source_type":"pr_branch_file","source":"web/net.js[98-108]"},{"source_type":"pr_branch_file","source":"web/net.js[113-125]"},{"source_type":"pr_branch_file","source":"web/index.html[365-373]"}]}},{"id":"07c384af-9671-437a-83c4-3b427ff9f947","title":"A leader change hides a valid join result","description":"`fromLeader()` checks a result against the receiver’s current `s.leader`, rather than the leader that decided the push. If the old time keeper resolves a join between devices that remain in the room and then leaves before its `joined` broadcast arrives, those devices elect a successor and discard the in-flight result.","category":"Correctness","action_level":"remediation_recommended","attribution_status":"pending","git_sha":"1306dc9550075866fb556726e87e1526b70fe4b9","location":{"file_path":"web/net.js","start_line":150,"end_line":151,"side":"right"},"evidence":{"citations":[{"source_type":"pr_branch_file","source":"web/net.js[33-52]"},{"source_type":"pr_branch_file","source":"web/net.js[110-116]"},{"source_type":"pr_branch_file","source":"web/net.js[127-153]"},{"source_type":"pr_branch_file","source":"web/index.html[364-369]"}]}},{"id":"4bf90ebe-18fb-43ef-8cbe-b845d9227735","title":"Join tests skip non-facing and edge taps","description":"The new join checks in `tests/two-browser.mjs` and `tests/net.test.mjs` start every join through direct `net.push` calls, so no test sends a tap from a strip or a tap position. Non-facing taps, endpoint positions and default keyboard positions are never exercised. Strip-based stacking and overlap are deferred by the author's stated scope, so this is a known gap rather than a defect in the shipped path, but a later change could join ineligible taps without any failing check.","category":"Maintainability","action_level":"remediation_recommended","attribution_status":"pending","git_sha":"1306dc9550075866fb556726e87e1526b70fe4b9","location":{"file_path":"tests/two-browser.mjs","start_line":67,"end_line":69,"side":"right"},"evidence":{"citations":[{"source_type":"ticket","source":"https://github.com/jtbinith/qodo-hack-2026/issues/68"},{"source_type":"pr_branch_file","source":"tests/two-browser.mjs[67-69]"},{"source_type":"pr_branch_file","source":"tests/net.test.mjs[59-72]"}]}}],"finding_state":{"complete":true,"introduced":["4209e603-e565-40bb-b9d9-7754cb40e5a2"],"resolved":["0f2994d4-afd4-4c73-88c3-95fb9f2b1a9a","e5fa90a2-f25f-4c2d-9c2e-0aabc032907d","c7362445-a6d5-4315-9baa-19adb76c1795"],"stil